==============================================================================
GV1 [G] why more n cannot REVERSE the verdict (named certificate)
==============================================================================
  A finite-n enumeration gives LOWER bounds on a limsup, never an upper bound.
  Ring 3 open needs lambda_Ur < 1.6 = an UPPER bound valid for ALL clusters.
  => no n can open it; only a structural bound |c_Gamma| <= C^n with C < 1.6 can,
     which is exactly what C06:187 hoped for and what n<=6 refutes.
  Cost of the full enumeration (measured: n=6 -> 7.218768e6 sets, 177 s DP):
    n= 7: ~1.860e+08 clusters  ->  level-set memory ~   26.04 GB   DP time ~     75.9 min
    n= 8: ~4.792e+09 clusters  ->  level-set memory ~  670.90 GB   DP time ~   1956.8 min
    n= 9: ~1.235e+11 clusters  ->  level-set memory ~17285.91 GB   DP time ~  50417.2 min
    n=10: ~3.181e+12 clusters  ->  level-set memory ~445375.10 GB   DP time ~1299010.7 min
  => n=7 is memory-marginal here (16.7 GB free, one 0.88 GB job running),
     n>=8 needs an orderly-generation (Redelmeier) enumerator in C. Not done.
